In the article at http://www.eweek.com/article2/0,3959,1054003,00.asp

they talk about the 2.6 kernel having a DM, but not a LVM.

What are they trying to say?  What is it they think is missing?

=3D=3D=3D Quote from the article
=20
According to Witham, a number of features that the development community=20
believes are not yet ready to be incorporated into the 2.6 kernel may very well =
be pushed to 2.7.

Among these features are support for complete Non-Uniform Memory Access=20
as well as an EVMS (Enterprise Volume Management System), which deals=20
with the difficult and controversial issue of volume management, Frye said,=20
adding that 2.6 would be better than 2.4 in terms of volume management even=20
without the EVMS.

Oracle Corp. and Red Hat Inc. officials have also previously called for volume=20
management. Wim Coekaerts, principal member of Oracle's technical staff, in=20
Redwood Shores, Calif., said: "We would like Linux to have a Logical Volume=20
Manager. The 2.6 kernel will have a device manager, but we need an LVM."

Paul Cornier, executive vice president of Red Hat, in Raleigh, N.C., agreed.

"Making a more generic cluster file system is important to us, as is an=20
industrial-strength Logical Volume Manager," Cornier said. "A distributed lock=20
manager completes things. This is functionality that needs to go into the =
operating=20
system but is unlikely to be found in the next [kernel] upgrade."

IBM's Frye said that there's clearly a need for an improved volume management=20
system and that Linux is not yet good enough in that regard.
